@startuml
!include https://raw.githubusercontent.com/proveo-ca/identity/main/proveo.puml
skinparam noteTextAlignment left
' purpose: what "all defs working" has to mean before release, and the ordered work to get there
' covers: e2e/ladder_test.go, e2e/idle_test.go, defs/*/harness.manifest
'
' ── THE BAR ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
' "Working" is not "it launched once for me". A def is GREEN when EVERY LADDER
' RUNG HOLDS on the sbx backend:
'
'   e2e/ladder_test.go, PROVEO_LADDER_TEST=1 PROVEO_LADDER_TARGET=<def>
'
' That is the release gate for all four defs, stated by the owner. The ladder is
' the right instrument for it because a rung failure NAMES ITS LAYER, so "not
' green" comes with an address rather than a symptom.
'
' e2e/idle_test.go stays complementary and is not the gate. It asks a different
' question — does the assembled run survive being left alone — and neither test
' implies the other: opencode passed every rung while its idle behaviour was
' unverified, and cursor passed idle long before its ladder was climbed. Worth
' running; not what blocks a release.
'
' Docker+egress results do NOT satisfy this bar. Every reported failure has been
' on sbx, and the fallback path passed while sbx died — a sweep on docker
' answered a question nobody asked.
'
' ── WHERE EACH DEF STANDS ──────────────────────────────────────────────────
'   claudecode   LADDER GREEN — all four rungs, base and browser images.
'   cursor       LADDER GREEN — all four rungs, base and browser images.
'   opencode     LADDER GREEN — all four rungs, after the `opencode opencode`
'                fix. Three of four defs now meet the gate.
'   cecli        ladder rung 2 FAILS. The one remaining blocker, and the
'                measurement is STALE: the image it ran against was built at
'                03:18 and the entrypoint fix landed at 10:02, so that climb
'                judged code that is no longer in the tree. Rebuild before
'                reading anything into it.
'
' ── THE BLOCKER ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
' cecli rung 2 — adding `-- cecli` as the sbx COMMAND — dies in ~3s with
' `/opt/cecli/bin/cecli: line 2: import: command not found`: bash falling back to
' INTERPRETING a Python entry point, which is what it does when execve returns
' ENOEXEC. Rung 1 held, so the image is exonerated; only the launch is broken.
'
' Ruled out already: proveo-harden (strips setuid/setgid only, never the exec
' bit), python3 being removed after the venv (nothing purges it, single stage),
' and our entrypoint failing to run (it runs — ENTRYPOINT is dumb-init --
' cecli-entrypoint, and the bash fallback is what prints those lines).
'
' AND FOUR MORE CAUSES ARE NOW ELIMINATED, by the probe rather than by argument:
'
'   id        uid=1000(cecli) — the running user, not a stranger to the file
'   file      -rwxr-xr-x, resolvable on PATH at /opt/cecli/bin/cecli
'   shebang   #!/opt/cecli/bin/python3
'   interp    a symlink resolving to /usr/bin/python3.13
'   runs      Python 3.13.5
'
' So the interpreter exists, is executable by the running uid, and works. A
' dangling symlink, a non-executable interpreter, a wrong shebang path and a uid
' that cannot traverse the directory are all ruled out by measurement.
'
' What remains is narrower and stranger: the file is perfectly executable, yet
' the session fell back to INTERPRETING it. That is either an invocation which
' bypasses the shebang, or a shebang the kernel rejects for a reason head(1)
' cannot show — a BOM or stray byte before `#!`. The probe now asks the kernel
' outright (execve of the resolved path) and dumps the first four bytes, which
' separates those two.
'
' Still unknown, and NOT to be guessed at — three theories died that way on the
' opencode hunt before the ladder settled it in 103 seconds. Should the probe
' ever be unavailable, this answers the same question by hand on a host with
' sbx:
'
'   sbx run -t proveo/cecli:local shell /tmp -- sh -c \
'     'ls -l /opt/cecli/bin/python3; readlink -f /opt/cecli/bin/python3; \
'      /opt/cecli/bin/python3 -V; head -1 /opt/cecli/bin/cecli; id'
'
' It separates a dangling venv symlink, a non-executable interpreter, a wrong
' shebang, and a uid that cannot traverse /opt/cecli/bin. If it is the symlink,
' `python3 -m venv --copies` is the fix and a rung should keep watch on it.
'
' THE LADDER NOW ASKS THIS ITSELF. probeLaunch runs that enquiry inside the dead
' rung's sandbox before teardown, because the sandbox outlives the session and a
' manual command run later finds it gone. Best-effort by construction: a probe
' that cannot run reports nothing rather than turning one failure into two.
'
' ── WHAT IS NOT COVERED BY EITHER SUITE ────────────────────────────────────
' Eight images are declared across four defs, and the suites reach five of them.
'
'   claudecode-solidity   no suite touches it. It builds FROM the claudecode
'                         image, so it inherits that layer's verdict, but the
'                         solidity toolchain on top is unmeasured.
'   cecli-browser         does not exist. The ladder SKIPPED rung 3 for cecli
'                         because the image is not built. Decide whether that is
'                         deliberate — cecli may simply not want one — and record
'                         the answer either way, because a skip that means
'                         "absent by design" and a skip that means "we forgot"
'                         are indistinguishable in the output.
'
' ── AND A RED THAT IS NOBODY'S FEATURE ─────────────────────────────────────
' internal/ptyproxy's two PTY tests fail roughly one run in three, at the same
' rate on a clean tree as on any branch. They spawn real ptys and do not survive
' -count=2 at all. Left alone they will redden the release CI at random and train
' everyone to re-run, which is how a real failure gets waved through.
title Plan — every def green before release

card "THE BAR (sbx, per def)" as BAR <<host>> {
  component "ladder: every rung holds" as L <<app>>
  component "idle: 5m at a prompt, zero input" as I <<app>>
}
card "claudecode" as CC <<app>>
card "cursor" as CU <<app>>
card "opencode" as OC <<app>>
card "cecli" as CE <<error>>
component "cecli rung 2 — ENOEXEC on the venv entry point" as BLOCK <<error>>
component "unmeasured: claudecode-solidity · cecli-browser" as GAP <<error>>
component "flaky: internal/ptyproxy PTY tests" as FLAKE <<error>>

BAR ARROW_MAIN CC
BAR ARROW_MAIN CU
BAR ARROW_MAIN OC
BAR ARROW_MAIN CE
CE ARROW_ERROR BLOCK
BAR ARROW_ERROR GAP
BAR ARROW_ERROR FLAKE

note bottom of BAR
  **The gate is the ladder.** A rung failure names
  its LAYER, so "not green" arrives with an
  address. Idle is complementary and asks a
  different question; it does not block.
end note

note bottom of BAR
  Docker+egress results do not satisfy it. Every
  reported failure has been on sbx, and the
  fallback passed while sbx died.
end note

note bottom of BLOCK
  Rung 1 held, so the IMAGE is fine and only the
  launch is broken. One command separates the
  remaining causes; do not add a fifth theory
  before running it.
end note

note bottom of GAP
  A skip that means "absent by design" and one
  that means "we forgot" look identical in the
  output. Record which cecli-browser is.
end note

note bottom of FLAKE
  ~1 run in 3, same on a clean tree. Left alone it
  trains everyone to re-run a red CI, which is how
  a real failure gets waved through.
end note
@enduml
